Step 3: Training a Model
For this first model, the point-level features are aggregated per segment using the mean, maximum, and minimum. A CART decision tree is trained because it is easy to interpret and gives a good starting point.

mean2 &lt;- \(x) mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)
max2  &lt;- \(x) max(x,  na.rm = TRUE)
min2  &lt;- \(x) min(x,  na.rm = TRUE)

tracks_smry &lt;- full_dataset |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  select(data, track_id, mode, steplength, timelag, speed, sinuosity) |&gt;
  group_by(data, track_id, mode) |&gt;
  summarise(
    across(
      c(steplength, timelag, speed, sinuosity),
      list(mean = mean2, max = max2, min = min2)
    )
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(mode = factor(mode)) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  select(-track_id)

tracks_training   &lt;- tracks_smry |&gt; filter(data == "training")   |&gt; select(-data)
tracks_testing    &lt;- tracks_smry |&gt; filter(data == "testing")    |&gt; select(-data)
tracks_validation &lt;- tracks_smry |&gt; filter(data == "validation") |&gt; select(-data)


library(rpart)
library(rpart.plot)

cart_model &lt;- rpart(mode ~ ., data = tracks_training, method = "class")

rpart.plot(cart_model, type = 2)




Figure 2: Decision tree trained on segment-level movement features.




The tree in Figure 2 shows that speed_mean is the most important variable for the first split. Segments with a mean speed below 2.5 m/s are mostly classified as walking (46% of all segments). Faster segments are split further using steplength_mean, steplength_max, and timelag_mean to separate bike, bus, car, subway, and train. Subway (4%) is separated using speed_max &gt;= 23, while train (1%) ends up in the deepest branch through a combination of high step length and high time lag.

Preparing Point-Level Training and Testing Data
No aggregation is applied here. Each row is one GPS point with its own feature values. Points at segment boundaries where features cannot be computed are removed.

feat_cols &lt;- c("speed", "acceleration", "sinuosity", "turn_angle")

point_data &lt;- full_dataset |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  select(data, mode, all_of(feat_cols)) |&gt;
  filter(if_all(all_of(feat_cols), ~ is.finite(.)))

point_train &lt;- point_data |&gt;
  filter(data == "training", !is.na(mode)) |&gt;
  select(-data) |&gt;
  mutate(mode = factor(mode))

point_test &lt;- point_data |&gt;
  filter(data == "testing", !is.na(mode)) |&gt;
  select(-data) |&gt;
  mutate(mode = factor(mode))

